#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f203c9bf-7d12-56db-aafe-6639162eae93" :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given Old Variables Through New Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "248c9a29-6698-5c8c-b674-0b0dc537edf0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8177" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/57718ef2e6ecd26138c4c6a1b817d1129ad95a7473bf2e7d1611fdc64391e5c8.png) express the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-x_2^2\\,$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/12e6df2cc03a95228ac8d54512772c914f3283f1f62d814f2e996de67c93a041.png)\n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}-(2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f2a5391-d8bb-5084-941a-bb787e8729f3" :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given New Variables Through Old Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "1c199cdf-ca23-53e8-8b5d-1ba43c842d41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8178" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/309c32ea89b3e3216e79962d305ef2611eeb691342ae07f7659aff52c218c5b3.png) express the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/7ea02af91b7b066c7aafa293882f6e2f9f81256fe8b0a30f33f2f4403142d928.png) \n\ngives the following expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for $x_1$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})(-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})+2(-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+8{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}_{1}^{2}+9{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161717 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4c239ba2-66c9-5170-8a5a-9f7071315dd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8179" :question/problem "Consider the matrices $A$ and $P$ below. Find the matrix of the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/8c8f1626eafc81b4700a0ddc015c210508d4db464970abc706831ea86659e8da.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}=[x_1, x_2]^T$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $\\mathbf{y}=[ y_1, y_2 ]^T$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = P\\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f203c9bf-7d12-56db-aafe-6639162eae93" :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given Old Variables Through New Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa7b6b87-808b-57a6-b80d-026db8c60039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358247} {:answer/id #uuid "21cdaee9-e992-5da6-a079-eb43f72bc68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-13{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-13{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358248} {:answer/id #uuid "26b238c7-55fc-5d28-a65a-2f2b4a35c679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358249} {:answer/id #uuid "6b373948-46e7-553b-93fc-ff7ebd7a3709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{y}_{1}^{2}-17{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-12{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358250} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bddca0-f01e-53e5-9675-62da47ff1737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b373948-46e7-553b-93fc-ff7ebd7a3709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{y}_{1}^{2}-17{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-12{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358250} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce33c585-a739-5f29-809e-e0931a30db47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "101b0e7a-cd79-5786-8173-8a6700413fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-140936" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})-3({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =8{y}_{1}^{2}-8{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}-3{y}_{1}^{2}-12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-12{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =7{y}_{1}^{2}-17{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-12{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f203c9bf-7d12-56db-aafe-6639162eae93" :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given Old Variables Through New Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb462dae-711c-5fe3-9e5c-b0f7c0462973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-9{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358254} {:answer/id #uuid "204dec2b-accd-5d60-8073-6e6dbf8ca840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358255} {:answer/id #uuid "8350b7a2-8738-55fc-ab39-c81e6d70523c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+9{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358256} {:answer/id #uuid "7634cbea-3f64-5494-99f0-0747f46503e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{y}_{1}^{2}-15{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-27{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358257} {:answer/id #uuid "90d20fde-86bc-5712-8d47-4aee1efb43e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{y}_{1}^{2}-30{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+27{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d20fde-86bc-5712-8d47-4aee1efb43e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{y}_{1}^{2}-30{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+27{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4953592-8ca3-5442-ad7d-1330f03f9c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20339293-778c-5bbb-946b-a6ff1065301d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140937" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  \n\ngives the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1})^{2}-4({y}_{1})(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})+3(-{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}^{2}-12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{y}_{1}^{2}-18{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+27{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =8{y}_{1}^{2}-30{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+27{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f2a5391-d8bb-5084-941a-bb787e8729f3" :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given New Variables Through Old Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1133b61b-1568-5b15-912b-d15af9380b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358261} {:answer/id #uuid "94b2f8c9-6f0b-556f-a20e-34be1f8da17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358262} {:answer/id #uuid "8bffbd28-1314-5e4d-adcb-b5d715ec772b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358263} {:answer/id #uuid "8e607860-ecb5-5d41-a52a-3f848791f3be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+8{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358264} {:answer/id #uuid "09c53798-4722-56f6-b361-8f516d66bc82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c53798-4722-56f6-b361-8f516d66bc82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358265} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff5100a-99ce-54e0-9d96-e7d247c2c4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43caec47-3f60-556e-94f0-5da8ae1b32dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140939" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{1}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2})({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})-({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e5b43d-4bb3-5773-8876-e02a6e70068c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358268} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa93528-9c85-5b3d-a104-35ef4909161f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358269} {:answer/id #uuid "41227c04-8102-5310-91b1-86a41d585c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358270} {:answer/id #uuid "0ffcc2ed-2f10-5d03-80f0-a1aadb2c9b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358271} {:answer/id #uuid "1c85d9b2-0841-5a20-9f5a-727f5ca87ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ffcc2ed-2f10-5d03-80f0-a1aadb2c9b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358271} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ead08f-a666-5db0-a4ab-f8f4f7b9d7fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f3efd2f-662c-53a4-b995-cf7a32bbae74" :question/math-academy-id "q-140941" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5e3f203-1a2a-506e-ab94-d2ba146845a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160807} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9b2a53-261b-5246-b5ba-d2da3b2544ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160808} {:answer/id #uuid "2af99e0b-bd0e-5925-ac51-3b4958451e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160809} {:answer/id #uuid "5126ad23-f9d0-5e11-b3d7-ab28b3f7db85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160810} {:answer/id #uuid "7b260542-b9ca-